## Database languages

Database languages are special-purpose languages, which allow one or more of the following tasks, sometimes distinguished as [sublanguages](/wiki/Sublanguage "Sublanguage"):

- [Data control language](/wiki/Data_control_language "Data control language") (DCL) – controls access to data;
- [Data definition language](/wiki/Data_definition_language "Data definition language") (DDL) – defines data types such as creating, altering, or dropping tables and the relationships among them;
- [Data manipulation language](/wiki/Data_manipulation_language "Data manipulation language") (DML) – performs tasks such as inserting, updating, or deleting data occurrences;
- [Data query language](/wiki/Data_query_language "Data query language") (DQL) – allows searching for information and computing derived information.

Database languages are specific to a particular data model. Notable examples include:

- [Data control language](/wiki/Data_control_language "Data control language") (DCL) – controls access to data;
- [Data definition language](/wiki/Data_definition_language "Data definition language") (DDL) – defines data types such as creating, altering, or dropping tables and the relationships among them;
- [Data manipulation language](/wiki/Data_manipulation_language "Data manipulation language") (DML) – performs tasks such as inserting, updating, or deleting data occurrences;
- [Data query language](/wiki/Data_query_language "Data query language") (DQL) – allows searching for information and computing derived information.

Database languages are specific to a particular data model. Notable examples include:

- SQL combines the roles of data definition, data manipulation, and query in a single language. It was one of the first commercial languages for the relational model, although it departs in some respects from [the relational model as described by Codd](/wiki/Codd%27s_12_rules "Codd's 12 rules") (for example, the rows and columns of a table can be ordered). SQL became a standard of the [American National Standards Institute](/wiki/American_National_Standards_Institute "American National Standards Institute") (ANSI) in 1986, and of the [International Organization for Standardization](/wiki/International_Organization_for_Standardization "International Organization for Standardization") (ISO) in 1987. The standards have been regularly enhanced since and are supported (with varying degrees of conformance) by all mainstream commercial relational DBMSs.[[35]](#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChapple2005-36)[[36]](#cite_note-IBM-sql-37)
- [OQL](/wiki/OQL "OQL") is an object model language standard (from the [Object Data Management Group](/wiki/Object_Data_Management_Group "Object Data Management Group")). It has influenced the design of some of the newer query languages like [JDOQL](/w/index.php?title=JDOQL&action=edit&redlink=1 "JDOQL (page does not exist)") and [EJB QL](/wiki/EJB_QL "EJB QL").
- [XQuery](/wiki/XQuery "XQuery") is a standard XML query language implemented by XML database systems such as [MarkLogic](/wiki/MarkLogic "MarkLogic") and [eXist](/wiki/EXist "EXist"), by relational databases with XML capability such as Oracle and Db2, and also by in-memory XML processors such as [Saxon](/wiki/Saxon_XSLT "Saxon XSLT").
